   "id": "math-0016",
   "bucket": "reasoning",
   "planned_path": "P5",
   "scion_answer": "We can use the binomial distribution formula to solve this problem. The probability of getting exactly $k$ heads in $n$ flips of an unfair coin with probability of heads $p$ is:\n\n$$P(X = k) = \\binom{n}{k} p^k (1-p)^{n-k}$$\n\nHere, $n = 5$, $k = 2$, and $p = \\frac{2}{3}$, so $1-p = \\frac{1}{3}$.\n\n**Step 1: Compute the binomial coefficient**\n\n$$\\binom{5}{2} = \\frac{5!}{2! \\cdot 3!} = \\frac{120}{2 \\cdot 6} = \\frac{120}{12} = 10$$\n\n**Step 2: Compute the probability factors**\n\n$$\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^2 = \\frac{4}{9}$$\n\n$$\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^3 = \\frac{1}{27}$$\n\n**Step 3: Multiply everything together**\n\n$$P(X = 2) = 10 \\cdot \\frac{4}{9} \\cdot \\frac{1}{27} = 10 \\cdot \\frac{4}{27} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{40}{81}$$\n\n$$\\boxed{\\dfrac{40}{81}}$$",
